course: dictionary
lesson: 2322
title: A crosswalk is a safe place to cross
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A crosswalk is a marked place on a road where people walk across, often with white stripes or traffic lights. In the USA and Canada, this is the word people use. In the UK it is called a zebra crossing or pedestrian crossing.
explain: Read the words around it to understand it. «At a painted crosswalk in the USA, drivers must yield, but some do not, so wait for cars to stop and make eye contact before crossing.» Yield means let you go first, and the sentence tells you a crosswalk is where cars should let people cross.
explain: Use the swap check. «Use the crosswalk» could become «use the zebra crossing» and still make sense, so you know they mean the same place. If your meaning fits the sentence, you have understood the word.
explain: Staying safe at a crosswalk means more than stepping onto it. Wait for the walk signal, look left, right and left again, and make sure the cars have stopped. Some drivers do not stop, so never walk out just because you can see a crosswalk.
fact: A crosswalk is a marked place on a road where people walk across.
fact: «Crosswalk» is the word used in the USA and Canada; in the UK it is a zebra crossing or pedestrian crossing.
fact: At a crosswalk, wait for cars to stop and make eye contact with the drivers, because some drivers do not yield (let you go first).
fact: Look left, right and left again before you cross, even at a crosswalk.
fact: A crossing guard is a person who helps people, especially children, cross the road safely.
exercise: «At a painted crosswalk in the USA, drivers must yield.» What is a crosswalk? | *A marked place on a road where people walk across | A type of traffic light for cars only | Sweets sold at the side of the road | A bus stop with a bench
exercise: «So wait for cars to stop and make eye contact with the drivers before you cross.» What should you do at a crosswalk? | Look only to the left | Run across quickly before the cars see you | *Wait for cars to stop and look at the drivers | Ride your bike up the inside of a truck
exercise: What is another name for a crosswalk used in the UK? | *Zebra crossing | Traffic light | Crossing guard | Pavement
test: «Some drivers do not yield.» What does yield mean? | Turn off the road | Honk your horn | Speed up | *Let you go first
test: Why should you look left, right and left again at a crosswalk? | To find your friends | To see the sun | To count the cars | *To check the cars have stopped before you cross
exam: «A toddler can dart into the road without warning.» Which safe rule protects a small child? | Let them run ahead of you | Cross without looking | *Hold their hand and keep them on the side away from the traffic | Let them cross the road alone
